J.A.I.S.S (Just A Intelligent Secure System)

Local Multi-Agent Hypervisor & Zero-Drift Architecture



Overview

Tonight's development session focused on expanding the local execution framework within C:\J.A.I.S.S by introducing automated agent synthesis, immutable historical ledgers, and strict semantic isolation.

Rather than bloating the core system with monolithic dependencies, this update establishes a lightweight governance and memory layer built entirely around modular JSON manifests and PowerShell automation.



Key Engineering Milestones Achieved

1. Agent Builder & Deployer Framework (Invoke-JAISSAgentBuilder)

Dynamic Synthesis: Implemented a PowerShell function that spins up task-specific agent profiles on demand with randomized unique identifiers (Agent_XXXX).

Automated Archival: Every executed agent automatically logs its performance notes, operational status, timestamps, and assigned tasks directly to a local deployment history ledger.

2. Workspace Hygiene & Zero-Drift Auditing

Root Cleanup: Configured automated scanning protocols to prune loose temporary artifacts (.log, .tmp, .bak) from the root directory, ensuring pristine environment hygiene.

Structural Isolation: Enforced strict directory separation across config/ (manifests and read-only reference maps) and ledger/ (execution history and historical milestones).

3. Read-Only Semantic Lineage Isolation

Context Preservation without Entropy: Established a dedicated read-only semantic lineage map (semantic_lineage.json) to silo early developmental concepts and architectural designators (such as early hypervisor and symbolic governance frameworks).

Execution Boundary: Ensured that historical designators act purely as passive reference metadata for holistic self-awareness, completely isolated from active runtime control loops to prevent operational drift.

